We may in passing however observe that it seems to us that it is possible to argue that there is a difference between the nature of the evidence which would be necessary to sustain a plea of waiver on the part of a person claiming certain benefits to accrue to him by waiver by another of his (that other's) rights and the nature of the evidence necessary to sustain a plea on the part of a person who claims to have waived the right. The same view of the effect of such a clause was takes by another bench of the Calcutta High Court in
Basanta Kumar v. Nabin Chandra, (C
). A different note however was struck by the Allahabad High Court in Ajudhia v. Kunjal, ILR 30 All 123 (G), where a Bench of that Court dissented from
Hurri Pershad v. Nasib Singh (E
), and
Jadab Chandra v. Bhairab Chandra (B
). In doing so, they referred to the remarks made in an earlier case decided by their own Court
